## Vercel Web Analytics Installation

Successfully installed and configured Vercel Web Analytics for the VitalQR Next.js application.

### Changes Made

#### 1. Installed @vercel/analytics Package
- Added `@vercel/analytics` version 2.0.1 to project dependencies
- Used npm to install the package (matching the project's package manager)
- Updated package.json and package-lock.json

#### 2. Configured Analytics in Root Layout
**Modified:** `app/app/layout.tsx`
- Added import statement: `import { Analytics } from '@vercel/analytics/next';`
- Added `<Analytics />` component inside the `<body>` tag, after the provider components
- Follows Next.js App Router best practices by placing Analytics at the root layout level

### Implementation Details

The implementation follows the official Vercel documentation (https://vercel.com/docs/analytics/quickstart):
- For Next.js App Router projects, the Analytics component should be imported from `@vercel/analytics/next`
- The component is placed in the root layout to track all page views across the application
- No additional configuration is required; the component will automatically track page views when deployed to Vercel

### Next Steps

To complete the setup:
1. Deploy the application to Vercel
2. Enable Web Analytics in the Vercel dashboard (Project Settings → Analytics → Enable)
3. After deployment, verify analytics are working by checking the Network tab for requests to `/_vercel/insights/*`

### Notes

- The Analytics component has no visual output and works automatically once deployed to Vercel
- The installation is framework-specific and uses the Next.js-optimized version of the package
- The component placement ensures all routes in the application are tracked
- No environment variables or additional configuration files were needed

### Pre-existing Issue

Note: There is a pre-existing syntax error in `app/emergency/[id]/page.tsx` that prevents the build from completing. This issue existed before the Analytics installation and is unrelated to the changes made. The Analytics implementation itself is correct and will work once the pre-existing issue is resolved.
